      "worked": "<strong>How many cables does a mesh of 5 computers need?</strong><br>Formula: number of links = n(n−1)/2<br>Here n = 5<br>= 5 × (5 − 1) / 2<br>= 5 × 4 / 2<br>= 20 / 2<br>= <strong>10 links</strong><br>And each computer needs n − 1 = <strong>4 I/O ports</strong>.<br><br>Now try n = 10: 10 × 9 / 2 = <strong>45 links</strong>. Doubling the computers gave more than four times the cable — this is exactly why full mesh is rare.",
      "mistake": "Do not confuse topology with line configuration. <strong>Line configuration</strong> is about how devices attach to <em>one link</em> (point-to-point or multipoint). <strong>Topology</strong> is the shape of the <em>whole network</em>. Also: a star does not mean the computers talk to each other directly — every message passes through the central hub or switch.",
      "examAnswer": "Line configuration refers to the way two or more devices are attached to a communication link. In a point-to-point configuration a dedicated link is provided between two devices and the entire capacity of the channel is reserved for them, whereas in a multipoint or multidrop configuration more than two devices share a single link, the capacity being shared spatially or temporally. Topology refers to the physical or logical arrangement of the devices in a network. In a bus topology all nodes are connected to a single backbone cable; in a star topology every node is connected by a dedicated link to a central hub or switch; in a ring topology each node is connected to two neighbouring nodes forming a closed loop; in a mesh topology every node has a dedicated link to every other node, requiring n(n−1)/2 links for n nodes; and a hybrid topology combines two or more of these. Star topology is the most commonly used in practice because faults are easily isolated, while mesh topology offers the greatest reliability at the greatest cost.",
      "remember": "Bus = one road. Star = one centre. Ring = one circle. Mesh = everyone to everyone. Mesh links = n(n−1)/2.",
      "why": "Topology decides the cost of cabling, how the network behaves when something breaks, and how hard it is to find the fault — the three questions any real network administrator faces.",
      "practice": "Draw all five topologies. Then calculate the number of links needed for a mesh network of 8 devices."
