      "title": "Functions of the Layers: The OSI Reference Model",
      "teacher": "Posting a parcel to Chennai involves you, the shop that packs it, the post office that addresses it, the truck, the railways... Nobody does the whole job, and nobody needs to understand the other's job. Networking is organised in exactly the same way — seven departments, each doing one thing. Learn the order once and you have it for life.",
      "explain": "The <strong>OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model</strong>, given by ISO, divides the work of networking into <strong>seven layers</strong>. Each layer performs one well-defined function, uses the services of the layer below it, and provides services to the layer above it. From bottom to top the layers are: <strong>Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "OSI was developed by <strong>ISO</strong> (International Organization for Standardization) as a reference model.",
        "<strong>Layer 1 — Physical</strong>: transmits raw bits over the medium; deals with voltages, cables, connectors, data rate.",
        "<strong>Layer 2 — Data Link</strong>: node-to-node (hop-to-hop) delivery; framing, physical/MAC addressing, error control, flow control, access control.",
        "<strong>Layer 3 — Network</strong>: source-to-destination delivery across multiple networks; logical (IP) addressing and <strong>routing</strong>.",
        "<strong>Layer 4 — Transport</strong>: process-to-process delivery; segmentation and reassembly, port addressing, end-to-end error and flow control (TCP, UDP).",
        "<strong>Layer 5 — Session</strong>: establishes, manages and terminates sessions; dialog control and synchronisation (checkpoints).",
        "<strong>Layer 6 — Presentation</strong>: translation of data formats, <strong>encryption/decryption</strong> and <strong>compression</strong>.",
        "<strong>Layer 7 — Application</strong>: services directly to the user — HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, file transfer, email.",
        "Data unit names: bits (L1), frames (L2), packets/datagrams (L3), segments (L4), messages/data (L5–L7).",
        "The practical model actually used on the Internet is <strong>TCP/IP</strong>, which has 4–5 layers."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "The three lower layers — moving the bits",
          "text": "The <strong>Physical</strong> layer is pure electronics and mechanics: what voltage is a 1, how many bits per second, which pin of the connector does what. The <strong>Data Link</strong> layer groups bits into <strong>frames</strong> and gets them safely across <em>one</em> link, using MAC addresses, error detection and flow control. The <strong>Network</strong> layer is the one that thinks about the whole journey — it adds logical (IP) addresses and chooses the route through many intermediate networks."
        },
        {
          "title": "The transport layer — the heart of the model",
          "text": "Layers 1–3 get data to the correct <em>computer</em>. The <strong>Transport</strong> layer gets it to the correct <em>program</em> on that computer, using <strong>port numbers</strong> — which is how your browser and your music app can both use the network without their data getting mixed up. It breaks long messages into segments, numbers them, reassembles them in order at the far end, and handles end-to-end error and flow control. TCP does all this reliably; UDP does it fast but without guarantees."
