      "worked": "<strong>Learn the order bottom-up with this sentence:</strong><br><em>Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away</em><br>P = <strong>P</strong>hysical (1)<br>D = <strong>D</strong>ata Link (2)<br>N = <strong>N</strong>etwork (3)<br>T = <strong>T</strong>ransport (4)<br>S = <strong>S</strong>ession (5)<br>P = <strong>P</strong>resentation (6)<br>A = <strong>A</strong>pplication (7)<br><br><strong>And top-down:</strong> <em>All People Seem To Need Data Processing</em> — Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical.",
      "mistake": "Do not mix up Layer 2 and Layer 3 addressing. The <strong>Data Link</strong> layer uses <strong>physical/MAC addresses</strong> and only gets data to the <em>next</em> device on the link. The <strong>Network</strong> layer uses <strong>logical/IP addresses</strong> and is responsible for the whole journey from original source to final destination. Also, encryption belongs to the <strong>Presentation</strong> layer, not the Application layer.",
      "examAnswer": "The Open Systems Interconnection model, proposed by the International Organization for Standardization, is a seven-layer reference model for network communication in which each layer performs a specific function, uses the services of the layer below and provides services to the layer above. The Physical layer transmits raw bits over the transmission medium and defines electrical and mechanical specifications. The Data Link layer is responsible for node-to-node delivery and performs framing, physical addressing, error control, flow control and access control. The Network layer provides source-to-destination delivery across multiple networks through logical addressing and routing. The Transport layer provides process-to-process delivery, performing segmentation and reassembly, port addressing and end-to-end error and flow control. The Session layer establishes, maintains and synchronises the interaction between communicating systems. The Presentation layer deals with the syntax and semantics of the information exchanged, including translation, encryption and compression. The Application layer provides services directly to the user, such as electronic mail, file transfer and web access.",
