          "text": "<strong>Twisted pair</strong> is the cheapest and easiest to install, and the twisting cancels out interference picked up equally by both wires; it is what connects the PCs in your lab. <strong>Coaxial cable</strong> has better shielding and much higher bandwidth, used historically for cable television and older Ethernet. <strong>Optical fibre</strong> transmits light instead of electricity, so it carries far more data, loses far less signal over distance, and cannot be disturbed by electrical noise or tapped easily — the reason all long-distance backbones are fibre."
