    {
      "title": "Switching: Circuit, Packet and Message",
      "teacher": "A network cannot give every pair of computers its own private cable — we saw that mesh gets impossible very fast. So the network must find a path through shared intermediate devices each time. There are three ways to do that, and the difference between them is basically: do you book the road in advance, or just start driving?",
      "explain": "<strong>Switching</strong> is the method a network uses to create a path from sender to receiver through intermediate switching nodes. The three techniques are <strong>circuit switching</strong> (reserve a whole path first), <strong>message switching</strong> (store and forward the whole message) and <strong>packet switching</strong> (break the message into packets and forward each one), with packet switching having two approaches: <strong>datagram</strong> and <strong>virtual circuit</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Circuit switching</strong>: a dedicated physical path is reserved for the whole conversation.",
        "Circuit switching has three phases: <strong>setup → data transfer → teardown (disconnect)</strong>.",
        "Once set up there is no per-packet delay, but capacity is wasted whenever nobody is speaking. Example: the classic telephone network.",
        "<strong>Message switching</strong>: the entire message is sent to a node, <strong>stored</strong> completely, then forwarded — 'store and forward'.",
        "Message switching needs no dedicated path, but each node needs large storage and the delay is very long. Now obsolete (telegram-style systems).",
        "<strong>Packet switching</strong>: the message is broken into small fixed-size <strong>packets</strong>, each carrying address information.",
        "<strong>Datagram approach</strong>: connectionless — each packet is treated independently, may take a different route, and may arrive <strong>out of order</strong> or not at all. Used by the Internet (IP).",
        "<strong>Virtual circuit approach</strong>: connection-oriented — a logical path is set up first and <em>all</em> packets follow it in order, identified by a <strong>VCI</strong> in the header.",
        "A virtual circuit is <em>logical</em>, not physical — the link is still shared with others, unlike circuit switching.",
        "Packet switching uses the network most efficiently, which is why the Internet is built on it."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Circuit switching — book the whole road",
          "text": "Before any data moves, the network reserves a complete physical path from sender to receiver and keeps it reserved until the call ends. Because the path is dedicated, data flows in order with a fixed, predictable delay — excellent for voice. The cost is waste: during every pause in your conversation the reserved path sits idle and no one else may use it. The old landline telephone network is the classic example."
        },
        {
          "title": "Message switching — store and forward",
          "text": "No path is reserved at all. The complete message is transmitted to the first intermediate node, which <strong>stores the whole message on disk</strong>, waits for a free outgoing link, and then forwards it to the next node — repeating until it reaches the destination. This uses the network efficiently and lets nodes set priorities, but every node needs huge storage and the total delay is long, so it is unsuitable for interactive or real-time traffic. It is now obsolete, surviving in ideas like store-and-forward email."
        },
        {
          "title": "Packet switching — the datagram approach",
          "text": "The message is chopped into small packets, each carrying the full destination address. Each packet is routed <strong>independently</strong>, so different packets of the same message may travel by different routes depending on traffic. They can therefore arrive <strong>out of order, delayed, or duplicated</strong>, and it is the transport layer at the destination (TCP) that must reorder them and ask for any missing ones. There is no setup phase — you simply start sending. This is how the Internet's IP layer works."
        },
        {
          "title": "Packet switching — the virtual circuit approach",
          "text": "Here a <strong>logical</strong> connection is established first, exactly like circuit switching's setup phase, and every packet of the message then follows that same pre-decided route in order. Each packet carries only a short <strong>virtual circuit identifier (VCI)</strong> rather than the full address, so headers are smaller and switching is faster. Packets arrive in order, but — crucially — the links along the route are still <em>shared</em> with other connections, so no bandwidth is wasted the way it is in true circuit switching. Frame Relay and ATM used this approach."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Circuit switching is booking a whole taxi for the day — it waits for you even while you are inside the shop. Packet switching (datagram) is sending ten friends to the same wedding by whatever bus each one finds; they all reach, but not together or in order. A virtual circuit is those ten friends all agreeing in advance to take the same bus route, one after another.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "",
          "Circuit Switching",
          "Message Switching",
          "Packet Switching"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
