      "worked": "<strong>Datagram vs Virtual Circuit — side by side</strong><br>You send a 3-packet message P1, P2, P3 from A to B.<br><br><strong>Datagram:</strong> No setup. Each packet carries the full address of B.<br>P1 takes route A–X–B · P2 takes route A–Y–B (X was busy) · P3 takes route A–X–B<br>P2 travels a faster route and arrives first → arrival order at B could be P2, P1, P3.<br>The destination must <strong>reorder</strong> them using sequence numbers.<br><br><strong>Virtual circuit:</strong> Setup packet first fixes the route A–X–B and assigns a VCI.<br>P1, P2, P3 all carry only the small VCI and all follow A–X–B.<br>Arrival order at B = P1, P2, P3 — <strong>always in order</strong>, no reordering needed.<br><br>Note the key point: in BOTH cases the links A–X and X–B are still shared with other users. Only true <em>circuit</em> switching reserves them exclusively.",
