      "examAnswer": "Switching is the technique by which a network establishes a path between a sender and a receiver through intermediate switching nodes. In circuit switching a dedicated physical path is established between the two end systems before any data is transferred, and the communication proceeds in three phases: circuit establishment, data transfer and circuit disconnection; the path remains reserved for the entire duration, giving a fixed delay but wasting capacity during idle periods, as in the traditional telephone network. In message switching no dedicated path is established; the entire message is transmitted to an intermediate node, stored there completely and then forwarded to the next node, a technique known as store and forward, which requires large storage at each node and introduces long delays. In packet switching the message is divided into small packets which are transmitted independently. The datagram approach is connectionless, each packet carrying the full destination address and being routed independently, so that packets may follow different routes and arrive out of order. The virtual circuit approach is connection oriented, a logical path being established before transmission so that all packets follow the same route in sequence, each carrying only a virtual circuit identifier. Packet switching makes the most efficient use of network resources and is the basis of the Internet.",
